  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "A parabola has a turning point located at $(2,-1)$. Its $x$-intercepts are $(-2,0)$ and $(6,0)$. Find the equation of the parabola in:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "turning point form",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=\\frac1{16}(x-2)^2-1$",
          "worked": "The turning point gives $y=a(x-2)^2-1$. Substitute $(-2,0)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=a(-2-2)^2-1\\\\0&=16a-1\\\\a&=\\frac1{16}\\\\y&=\\frac1{16}(x-2)^2-1.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "intercept form",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=\\frac1{16}(x+2)(x-6)$",
          "worked": "The $x$-intercepts give $y=a(x+2)(x-6)$. Substitute $(2,-1)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}-1&=a(2+2)(2-6)\\\\-1&=-16a\\\\a&=\\frac1{16}\\\\y&=\\frac1{16}(x+2)(x-6).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
